 I'm a very  inductive learner I like to look at  texts and conversations to kind of  figure out what's going on and look at  the pieces and go ah this piece must be  the verb this must be the whatever I  like to figure things out and that works  well with this book because the  beginning of each chapter and it will  have like like two or three different  conversations or texts so if you look at  this one let's go one conversation and  it's  got another one then it's got another  one and then after that it's got a text  and then after that it has the  vocabulary and after the vocabulary then  it has that's the kanji then after that  it has the grammar points and then it  has a grammar exercises so the the texts  and the conversations are separate from  all of the the breakdown and the  explanation and the exercises those come  after the texts so for me that's fine I  like to look at the texts and the  conversations and then flip back to the  explanations when I need it and then I  can figure out the other things on my  own to whatever extent I can then I can  just flip back if I need an explanation  some people like to have the explanation  right up front in their face from the  beginning they're more deductive  learners they like to have it explained  to them before they read the text or at  the same time so if you are like that  you might not like this book that much  but have a look at it I really liked it  and this was my favorite book ever so  another one at the intermediate level  was this one which I didn't like I  didn't have as much fun with it but it  was useful it taught me a lot it's this  one  chook you kahramana bow Nyong'o that  means japanese from the intermediate  level that's the name that's the meaning  of this title but this one is completely  in Japanese this other one an integrated  approach the one I showed you before  this has a lot of English in it and has  translations and explanations this one  is all in Japanese so this one is a bit  more